README-CIO -- the starred programs, and the five modules that serve them=======================================================================366 of the names in DOC/INDEX carry a star.  A star means the program needsone of Microware's runtime trap modules: `cio` for nearly all of them, andfor some `math`, `math881`, `csl` or `csl020`.THEY RUN HERE.  All five modules are on this disk, in CMDS, included withMicroware's permission -- SOURCES.txt records the exchange.  You need donothing, supply nothing, and fetch nothing.  The star tells you what aprogram depends on, not what it lacks.This file explains what the star means, and what to do in the two caseswhere you might want a different arrangement.Why so many programs need it----------------------------`cc -qixm` -- the C library in a shared trap handler rather than linked intoevery binary -- was the normal way to build for OS-9, and it is why a starredprogram is often a fraction of the size of a trap-free one.  Much of thiscollection was rebuilt trap-free (`-qm`) so it would run with nothing loadedat all; 570 of the 942 programs under CMDS need no module whatever.  The restwere left as their authors built them.A program that cannot find its trap handler stops at once with    **** Can't install trap handler ****That is the message to recognise.  It is not a broken program, and on thisdisk you should never see it -- if you do, the five modules have been removedor the execution directory is not this disk's CMDS.The list was MEASURED, by running every program on a disk with the fivemodules taken away and matching that message.  It was never derived fromlooking for the string `cio` inside a binary: that proxy is wrong in bothdirections, and this collection has the scars to prove it -- `vi_nocio`contains the string and does not need the module, while `cyberwar`, `gnuchess`and `g` lack it and do.If you would rather use your own--------------------------------You run OS-9, so you have these modules already, and you may prefer your copy-- a different edition, or one matched to your processor.  Two ways:1. KEEP YOUR OWN /dd.  Point os9exec at your system disk and mount this one   alongside, so /dd stays yours and the collection is simply another drive:       OS9DISK=/path/to/your/os9disk  OS9H1=/path/to/freeware/dd  os9exec shell       chx /h1/CMDS   Your `cio` is found first, from your own /dd/CMDS.2. REPLACE THE COPIES HERE.  Overwrite them in this disk's CMDS:       copy /h1/CMDS/cio  /dd/CMDS/cio       copy /h1/CMDS/math /dd/CMDS/math   The disk you end up with then contains modules from your own SDK, so keep   that copy for your own use; the collection as published stays as it is.`math` is needed only by the programs that do floating point -- seeDOC/README-FLOATINGPOINT, which also explains when you want `math881`instead.DOC/README-RUNNING covers where the collection should be mounted, and thehandful of programs that look for files under /h0.If you take the modules away----------------------------Someone redistributing a subset, or testing what is genuinely free-standing,may want the disk without them.  Remove all five from CMDS and 570 of the 942programs still run -- that is the whole point of the rebuild work.  The 372starred files stop with the message above until a licensed `cio` is put back.Where a starred program mattered, there is usually a trap-free alternativealready here:    starred           use instead    -------           -----------    *ed  *emacs       vi, sedt, sed    *vi_cio           vi (the EFFO build)    *ksh              bash, sh, gshell    *zoo              lha, tar, gzip    *fgrep            grep    *ckermit          kermit    *cat.cio          cat    *rcs and friends  no free replacement on this diskDOC/INDEX marks every one of them, so you can tell before you try.